{
 "path": "arc-divider-recolor/SKILL.md",
 "edits": [
  {
   "oldText": "description: Solve ARC grids split by a solid divider, where demonstrations identify which panel supplies the output and how its cells are mapped.",
   "newText": "description: Solve ARC grids split by a solid divider by identifying the source panel, orientation, crop, or cellwise relation."
  },
  {
   "oldText": "# Infer divider-panel outputs\n\n1. In every demonstration, locate the solid divider and measure the panels. Compare output dimensions and cell patterns against each panel (including rotations/reflections only when evidence supports them); determine whether output copies one panel, transforms it, or compares corresponding cells. Do not assume the lower or first panel is selected from orientation alone.\n2. Derive the mapping and any cross-panel rule cell by cell from demonstrations. Test every output cell, including cases where candidate inputs differ; reject any rule that misses even one location. Distinguish the candidate's exact condition (for example, a particular color pairing) from merely being in a panel, and track row/column offsets, crop, orientation, and panel dimensions explicitly. If more than one rule fits or none fits, request another demo rather than guessing.\n3. Apply only the fully verified rule to the test. Construct the exact demonstrated output dimensions and cell values; independently check each qualifying and nonqualifying position and the separator exclusion before submitting.",
   "newText": "# Infer divider-panel outputs\n\n1. Locate the solid divider and record panel dimensions. Compare output dimensions and patterns against each panel; determine whether it copies a panel, transforms it, or compares corresponding cells. Allow rotations or reflections only when demos support them; never select a panel from orientation alone.\n2. Track crop, orientation, row/column offsets, and panel dimensions explicitly. For a cellwise relation, identify the exact qualifying condition (such as a color pairing), not merely panel membership, and account for every output position. If competing mappings remain, use the discriminating-demo and verification procedure in `arc-stream-actions` rather than guessing.\n3. Apply the established mapping to the test, excluding the separator. Use `arc-stream-actions` for output construction and final checks."
  }
 ]
}

{
 "path": "arc-rectangle-noise/SKILL.md",
 "edits": [
  {
   "oldText": "description: Solve ARC grids with rectangular frames and enclosed regions, or noisy solid rectangular fields; infer fills and colors from demonstrations.",
   "newText": "description: Solve ARC grids with rectangular frames, enclosed regions, or noisy solid rectangular fields by inferring exact change conditions."
  },
  {
   "oldText": "# Infer rectangular-region transformations\n\n1. Compare input and output cell by cell. Identify solid fields, frame boundaries, enclosed interiors, anomalous pixels, and any repeated or aligned patterns; distinguish a hollow frame from a noisy solid field. Record every changed cell and relevant unchanged controls rather than narrating tentative interpretations.\n2. Infer the exact condition for each change: which structure, color, position, alignment, or relation qualifies, and the replacement value. Check the rule against all demonstrated changes and invariants, including apparent exceptions; do not extrapolate from one changed cell or infer a broad fill/recolor rule from a sparse example. If competing rules remain, request another demo.\n3. Apply only a rule that accounts for every demo change to the test grid. Preserve all cells not selected by the verified condition, then check dimensions and each changed/unchanged cell before submitting. When demos do not establish a rule, request a demo instead of submitting a guess.",
   "newText": "# Infer rectangular-region transformations\n\n1. Compare input and output cell by cell. Identify solid fields, frame boundaries, enclosed interiors, anomalous pixels, and repeated or aligned patterns; distinguish a hollow frame from a noisy solid field. Record changed cells and relevant unchanged controls.\n2. Infer the exact condition for each change—the qualifying structure, color, position, alignment or relation—and replacement value. Check all changes and invariants, including apparent exceptions. Do not generalize from one changed cell or a sparse example. For unresolved competing rules, use the discriminating-demo procedure in `arc-stream-actions`.\n3. Apply the established rule, preserving unselected cells. Use `arc-stream-actions` for output construction and final checks."
  }
 ]
}

{
 "path": "arc-stream-actions/SKILL.md",
 "edits": [
  {
   "oldText": "description: Solve Continual-ARC tasks by validating grid transformations against demos, choosing requests strategically, and obeying the action protocol.",
   "newText": "description: Solve Continual-ARC tasks by choosing demos, validating grid transformations, constructing exact outputs, and following the action protocol."
  },
  {
   "oldText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Identify the transformation by comparing every input/output cell, dimensions, preserved regions, and changed cells. Follow `arc-divider-recolor` for divider-panel tasks and `arc-rectangle-noise` for rectangular frames/fields. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not establish the general rule or prove the test is unchanged. If the test lacks an apparent divider or other defining structure seen in demos, do not force it into that task family or guess a split; request a demo to establish how the transformation applies.",
   "newText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Compare input/output cells, dimensions, preserved regions, and changes. For divider-panel tasks, read `arc-divider-recolor`; for rectangular frames or fields, read `arc-rectangle-noise`. An unchanged demo constrains only that example, not the general rule or test. If a defining structure seen in demos is absent from the test, do not force the task into that family or guess a split; request a demo to establish applicability."
  }
 ]
}
